<wolsok-blog-figure [figure]="figures.walls" />

<p>
  <a href="https://bacteria-wars.wolsok.de">Bacteria Wars</a> is a real-time
  strategy game in the browser: two colonies grow across a petri dish and fight
  for it, sixty ticks a second. Since October two players can fight each other
  online. That worked fine at my desk. Then players reported a familiar
  sentence:
  <em>“Could not connect. Try again - on the same Wi-Fi it works best.”</em> Two
  phones on mobile data could not play each other. And, the irony, neither could
  two devices on the same Wi-Fi.
</p>
<p>
  This article is about the fix: a TURN relay. What problem it solves, what a
  TURN server is, how the game is wired to one without ever calling a backend
  function, and the one surprise that killed every relayed match after ten
  minutes.
</p>

<h2>Peer to peer, in theory</h2>
<p>
  An online match runs in <strong>lockstep</strong>: both devices run the same
  deterministic simulation with the same seed, and only the players’ inputs
  travel. Each input is played six ticks (100 ms) after it was made, on both
  devices, so it usually arrives before the other side needs it. That is a few
  dozen bytes sixty times a second, about 6 MB for a ten-minute match. No game
  server needed: the two browsers talk directly over a
  <strong>WebRTC data channel</strong>.
</p>
<p>
  To open that channel, the devices first have to swap a description of
  themselves (an SDP <em>offer</em> and <em>answer</em>, including every network
  address they could be reached at). The game does that through Firestore: the
  host writes its offer into a room document
  <code>rooms/&#123;code&#125;</code>, the guest reads it by the six-character
  code and writes its answer back. After that Firestore is out of the picture.
</p>
<p>
  “Directly” is the catch. Most devices on the internet cannot be reached
  directly at all.
</p>

<h2>What a NAT does to an incoming packet</h2>
<wolsok-blog-figure [figure]="figures.nat" />
<p>
  Your phone has a private address like <code>10.23.4.5</code>. The internet
  sees the public address of a <strong>NAT</strong> (network address
  translation) in front of it: your router at home, or a carrier-grade NAT
  shared by thousands of phones on mobile data. When the phone sends a UDP
  packet out, the NAT picks a public port, remembers the mapping and lets the
  answers back in. A packet from anybody else to that port is dropped.
</p>
<p>
  For two devices that each sit behind a NAT, that is a deadlock: the first
  packet of either one looks unsolicited to the other’s NAT. WebRTC solves it
  with <em>hole punching</em>: both learn their public address and port, swap
  them in the offer and answer, and send packets to each other at the same time.
  Each outgoing packet opens a mapping that lets the other’s packets in.
</p>
<p>
